          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A medical college receives a body from a government hospital where no relative claimed it within 72 hours. Under which legal provision is this transfer authorised?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Transplantation of Human Organs Act, 1994</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The THOA 1994 governs voluntary body donation and prohibits commercial transactions involving human bodies. It does not cover the transfer of unclaimed bodies to medical colleges.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Indian Medical Council Act</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The Medical Council Act regulates medical education standards and professional conduct. It does not authorise the transfer of unclaimed bodies for anatomical study.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The relevant State Anatomy Act</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>State Anatomy Acts, such as the Tamil Nadu Anatomy Act of 1982, specifically authorise medical colleges to receive unclaimed bodies from government hospitals, prisons, and similar institutions after a defined waiting period.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Code of Criminal Procedure</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The CrPC deals with criminal law procedures, including inquests for suspicious deaths, but does not authorise the transfer of unclaimed bodies to anatomy departments.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Indian anatomy departments work under two legal layers: the THOA 1994 for voluntary donation, and State Anatomy Acts (e.g., Tamil Nadu Anatomy Act, 1982) for transfer of unclaimed bodies from government institutions."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Understanding which law applies to each cadaver source — voluntary donation under THOA versus unclaimed bodies under State Anatomy Acts — is essential for ethical anatomy practice."
                }
              ],
