          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A medical student photographs a cadaver during dissection and shares the image in a study group chat. According to the National Medical Commission, this action is classified as:</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Acceptable if used solely for educational discussion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The NMC does not make an exception for educational intent. Any photography of cadavers violates the donor's dignity and the family's right to privacy, regardless of purpose.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Professional misconduct that can result in expulsion</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The NMC explicitly classifies photographing cadavers or posting such images on social media as professional misconduct. Students in India have faced expulsion for this, as it violates the donor's dignity and the family's right to privacy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A minor infraction requiring a verbal warning</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is not treated as a minor matter. The NMC considers cadaver photography a serious violation of professional ethics, with consequences as severe as expulsion from medical college.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A legal offence only if the donor's identity is revealed</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The prohibition applies regardless of whether the donor's identity is disclosed. The act of photographing a cadaver itself constitutes professional misconduct under NMC guidelines.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Photographing cadavers or posting images on social media is classified as professional misconduct by the NMC, regardless of intent. Students have been expelled in India for doing this."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The trust donors place in medical education demands absolute respect — photography violates that trust regardless of the student's intent."
                }
              ],
